/// \defgroup grp_w2_win32_input Win32 keyboard device and cursor clipping
/// \ingroup grp_gameclient
/// \brief The DirectInput 8 keyboard behind TheKeyboard, and the helper that
///        confines the mouse cursor to the game window.
///
/// Two small pieces of the Windows device layer under the game client's
/// input:
/// - DirectInputKeyboard is the keyboard BFME 2 uses. The engine-side
///   `Keyboard` base class keeps the key state and turns key events into
///   messages; this subclass supplies the events from a DirectInput 8
///   keyboard device and answers the Caps Lock query from Windows.
/// - clipCursorToClient restricts the Windows cursor to the main window's
///   client area. Full-screen display set-up and the full-screen device
///   restore after alt-tab call it.
///
/// \par Where key events go
/// GameClient::init creates the keyboard through the game client's keyboard
/// factory virtual, which the W3D game client implements by allocating a
/// DirectInputKeyboard (the ledger identifies that slot as Zero Hour's
/// `createKeyboard`) and stores the result in `TheKeyboard`. It then calls
/// DirectInputKeyboard::init and names the object "TheKeyboard". Once per
/// client frame GameClient::update calls the keyboard's update, which
/// gathers the pending key events (in Zero Hour by calling
/// DirectInputKeyboard::getKey until it returns `KEY_NONE`, or `KEY_LOST`,
/// after which it resets the key state and stops for that update), and
/// then Keyboard::createStreamMessages, which appends a raw
/// key-down or key-up message for each event not yet consumed to
/// `TheMessageStream`. MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage
/// matches those raw messages against the key bindings in `TheMetaMap`. The
/// GameClient destructor deletes the keyboard. The main window procedure,
/// `WndProc`, calls Keyboard::resetKeys when the window gains or loses
/// focus. \ref sub_gameclient describes the message stream as a whole.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing in this group reads game data, and none of it can be changed
///   without code. Key bindings are data, but not here: `CommandMap` INI
///   blocks fill `TheMetaMap`, and BFME 2's GameEngine::init loads
///   `CommandMap.ini` during start-up, after creating `TheMetaMap`.
/// - Keyboard and cursor handling is per machine, on the client side of the
///   engine. It holds no logic state, so changing it cannot desynchronise a
///   multiplayer game as long as the same game messages come out of the
///   translators.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The keyboard is read through DirectInput 8. Every DirectInput call
///   made for the keyboard is in DirectInputKeyboard (and in its
///   `openKeyboard`), so a new keyboard backend replaces this one class and
///   the factory slot that creates it.
/// - The engine's key values are DirectInput keyboard scan codes. A backend
///   built on window messages, raw input or a portable library has to
///   translate its keys into that numbering, because the key-name table is
///   indexed by it and the `CommandMap` key bindings store and compare key
///   values in that numbering (see DirectInputKeyboard::getKey).
/// - Cursor confinement is plain Win32 (`GetClientRect`, `ClientToScreen`,
///   `ClipCursor`) on the main window handle, see clipCursorToClient.


/// \class DirectInputKeyboard
/// \ingroup grp_w2_win32_input
/// \brief The Win32 keyboard: a `Keyboard` whose key events come from a
///        DirectInput 8 keyboard device.
///
/// `Keyboard` is the engine's device-independent keyboard. It owns the key
/// state, the modifier flags, auto-repeat and the translation into raw key
/// messages, and it leaves two virtual functions to a device class: getKey,
/// which returns one buffered key event per call, and getCapsState, which
/// reports Caps Lock. DirectInputKeyboard fills both, extends init to open
/// the device and the destructor to close it, and overrides update only to
/// call the base version. Its virtual table also replaces the base class's
/// reset entry with a body that only forwards to Keyboard::reset, which is
/// empty. Zero Hour has the same override. In the BFME 2 build the linker
/// merged this body with identical code from an unrelated class, so its
/// address is shared with that other function.
///
/// The class adds two members: the DirectInput 8 interface and the keyboard
/// device created from it. Both start out null. `openKeyboard`, which
/// DirectInputKeyboard::init calls, creates them, and
/// DirectInputKeyboard::closeKeyboard releases them. While the device is
/// null, DirectInputKeyboard::getKey reports that there are no keys, so a
/// keyboard whose device could not be opened is silent rather than broken.
/// A device that was created but could not be acquired is kept, and getKey
/// keeps trying to acquire it.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// There is one instance per process. GameClient::init creates it through the
/// W3D game client's keyboard factory and stores it in `TheKeyboard`; the
/// GameClient destructor deletes it, which releases DirectInput through
/// DirectInputKeyboard::~DirectInputKeyboard. Other code reaches it only
/// through `TheKeyboard` and the `Keyboard` interface, for example the
/// Shift test used by the in-game UI and the modifier flags that mouse
/// messages carry.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The class is the whole DirectInput dependency of keyboard input. To
/// replace it, derive another class from `Keyboard` and create it from the
/// game client's keyboard factory instead. The contract it has to keep:
/// - getKey fills one event per call, in the order the keys were pressed,
///   and reports the end of the queue with `KEY_NONE`;
/// - when DirectInput reports lost input or an unacquired device and the
///   device can be acquired again, getKey reports `KEY_LOST`. Zero Hour's
///   base class (`updateKeys`) then calls resetKeys, which clears its key
///   buffer and state, and stops reading for that update, so the remaining
///   buffered events are read on the next one. The source comment's intent
///   to "loop again" is not what the loop does. How BFME 2's restructured
///   base class handles `KEY_LOST` is not confirmed;
/// - key values use DirectInput's scan-code numbering;
/// - getCapsState reports the Caps Lock toggle, which the constructor also
///   copies into the modifier flags.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The names are Zero Hour's (`Win32DIKeyboard.h` and `Win32DIKeyboard.cpp`
/// in the Zero Hour source). They were given to the BFME 2 bodies by body
/// shape and by position in the class's virtual table, whose getKey and
/// getCapsState entries fill the two pure virtual slots of `Keyboard`'s
/// table. The bodies are Zero Hour's as a release build compiles them,
/// without debug logging or assertions.
/// \see Keyboard::createStreamMessages, Keyboard::resetKeys,
///      MetaEventTranslator::translateGameMessage


/// \fn DirectInputKeyboard::DirectInputKeyboard(void)
/// \brief Constructs the keyboard with no DirectInput objects and seeds the
///        Caps Lock modifier from Windows.
///
/// Runs the `Keyboard` constructor, sets both DirectInput pointers to null
/// and then copies the current Caps Lock toggle into the base class's
/// modifier flags: the Caps Lock bit is set when Windows reports the key
/// toggled on (`GetKeyState(VK_CAPITAL)`, low bit) and cleared otherwise.
/// The device itself is not opened here; DirectInputKeyboard::init does
/// that.
///
/// Its only caller is the W3D game client's keyboard factory, which
/// GameClient::init runs.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::init, DirectInputKeyboard::getCapsState


/// \fn DirectInputKeyboard::~DirectInputKeyboard(void)
/// \brief Releases the DirectInput keyboard device and interface, then
///        destroys the `Keyboard` part.
///
/// Calls DirectInputKeyboard::closeKeyboard, so destroying the keyboard
/// gives the device back to DirectInput whether or not it was ever opened
/// successfully. The GameClient destructor deletes `TheKeyboard`, which
/// makes this the normal shutdown path for keyboard input.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::closeKeyboard


/// \fn void DirectInputKeyboard::init(void)
/// \brief Initialises the base keyboard and then opens the DirectInput
///        keyboard device.
///
/// Calls Keyboard::init first, which prepares the key names and the frame
/// counter of the base class, then `openKeyboard`, which creates the
/// DirectInput objects. GameClient::init calls it once, right after
/// creating the keyboard.
///
/// \par How opening can fail
/// `openKeyboard` creates the DirectInput 8 interface and the system
/// keyboard device, then sets the keyboard data format, a foreground,
/// non-exclusive cooperative level on the main window and an event buffer
/// of 256 entries (the same constant as Zero Hour), and finally acquires
/// the device.
/// - If any of the set-up steps fails (creating DirectInput, creating the
///   device, or setting the data format, the cooperative level or the
///   buffer size), it calls DirectInputKeyboard::closeKeyboard and returns,
///   and the keyboard is left without a device.
/// - The result of the final acquire is ignored. If it fails, the device is
///   kept, and DirectInputKeyboard::getKey acquires it on a later call.
///   Zero Hour's source checks this result but deliberately does not close
///   the keyboard, noting that acquiring can fail in windowed mode and
///   succeed later.
///
/// Nothing is reported to the caller in either case.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::closeKeyboard, DirectInputKeyboard::getKey


/// \fn void DirectInputKeyboard::update(void)
/// \brief Per-frame update; only forwards to Keyboard::update.
///
/// The override adds nothing in BFME 2: the body is a single call to
/// Keyboard::update, which gathers the frame's key events. In Zero Hour
/// that happens through DirectInputKeyboard::getKey; the BFME 2 call site
/// is not confirmed. Zero Hour's source has the same override, with a
/// disabled block that would have flushed the device's buffer.
/// GameClient::update calls it once per client frame, before
/// Keyboard::createStreamMessages.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::getKey


/// \fn Bool DirectInputKeyboard::getCapsState(void)
/// \brief Reports whether Caps Lock is toggled on.
///
/// Reads the toggle from Windows (`GetKeyState(VK_CAPITAL)`, low bit), not
/// from the DirectInput device, which reports only presses and releases,
/// not the state of the lock. It implements a pure virtual
/// function of `Keyboard`. Zero Hour's base class calls it when it resets
/// the key state (to restore the Caps Lock modifier) and when it turns a key
/// into a printable character; whether BFME 2's base class calls it in the
/// same places is not confirmed.
/// \return True when Caps Lock is on.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::DirectInputKeyboard


/// \fn void DirectInputKeyboard::getKey(KeyboardIO *key)
/// \brief Reads at most one buffered key event from the DirectInput device.
///
/// This is how keys reach the engine: the base class calls it repeatedly
/// while it updates, until it reports that no key is left (or until it
/// reports lost input, after which the base class resets and stops for that
/// update). In Zero Hour
/// that loop is the base class's `updateKeys`. BFME 2 restructures the
/// base-class update, so this description of the loop comes from Zero
/// Hour; the BFME 2 call site is not confirmed.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The record is first cleared to "no key": key `KEY_NONE`, sequence 0.
///   The state and status fields are written only when an event is
///   returned.
/// - With no device it returns at once. Otherwise it acquires the device on
///   every call (acquiring an already acquired device succeeds) and, only
///   if that acquire succeeds, asks DirectInput for one buffered event. A
///   successful read that finds the queue empty leaves "no key".
/// - If the acquire or the read reports lost input or an unacquired device
///   (the source comment names lost focus as the case), it tries to acquire
///   again. When that succeeds the key is set to `KEY_LOST` to say that
///   events were missed (the source comment's intent is to tell the caller
///   to loop again; Zero Hour's `updateKeys` instead calls resetKeys, which
///   clears its key buffer, and ends the read for that update; the BFME 2
///   caller's response is not confirmed). When it fails the record stays
///   "no key".
/// - Any other result leaves "no key". That includes a first acquire that
///   fails for another reason, in which case no event is requested. One
///   such case is DirectInput refusing to acquire a foreground-only device
///   (the cooperative level `openKeyboard` sets) while the window is in the
///   background.
/// - For an event, the key value is the low byte of DirectInput's object
///   offset. Under DirectInput's standard keyboard data format, which Zero
///   Hour's `openKeyboard` passes, that offset is the key's scan code.
///   Zero Hour's key definitions (`KeyDefs.h`) use those scan codes as the
///   engine's key values, and BFME 2's Keyboard::initKeyNames fills its
///   key-name table at the same indices. The state is
///   `KEY_STATE_DOWN` when the high bit of the event data (0x80) is set and
///   `KEY_STATE_UP` otherwise, assigned rather than combined with old bits.
///   The sequence is DirectInput's sequence number, which orders events;
///   DirectInput's time stamp is not used. The status is set to unused, so
///   the event has not been consumed yet.
///
/// \param key [out] The record to fill. Must not be null; it is not checked.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A replacement must keep the three outcomes: an event, `KEY_NONE` for an
/// empty queue, and `KEY_LOST` when the device reported lost input and was
/// acquired again. It must also report keys in DirectInput's scan-code
/// numbering. Because the device is acquired again on each call, keyboard
/// input resumes by itself once the window is back in front; a replacement
/// needs its own equivalent of this recovery.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::update, Keyboard::resetKeys,
///      Keyboard::createStreamMessages


/// \fn void DirectInputKeyboard::closeKeyboard(void)
/// \brief Releases the DirectInput keyboard device and the DirectInput
///        interface, leaving both pointers null.
///
/// The device is unacquired and released first, then the DirectInput 8
/// interface is released. Each step is skipped when its pointer is already
/// null, so the function is safe to call more than once and after a partial
/// open. It has two callers: the destructor, and `openKeyboard`, which
/// calls it to clean up when one of its set-up steps fails (but not when
/// only the final acquire fails, see DirectInputKeyboard::init). After it
/// runs, DirectInputKeyboard::getKey reports no keys.
/// \see DirectInputKeyboard::~DirectInputKeyboard, DirectInputKeyboard::init


/// \file W3DDisplayClipCursor.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_win32_input
/// \brief Confines the cursor to the main window's client area.
///
/// The unit holds one free function, clipCursorToClient, which the W3D
/// display calls in full-screen mode. It is a BFME addition: BFME 1 has the
/// same function, and Zero Hour has none.


/// \fn void clipCursorToClient(void)
/// \brief Clips the mouse cursor to the client area of the main game
///        window.
///
/// Keeps the cursor from leaving the game window. It has two callers, and
/// both call it only in full-screen mode:
/// - W3DDisplay::init, after the Direct3D device has been created. In a
///   window it instead calls WW3D::Set_Ext_Swap_Interval(0) (which sets the
///   device's full-screen presentation interval to immediate and then
///   resets the Direct3D device) and does not clip; full screen, it clips
///   the cursor.
/// - Reset_D3D_Device, when the game becomes active again (`WndProc` calls
///   it on `WM_ACTIVATEAPP`): after restoring the full-screen device it
///   clips the cursor again. The call depends on the result of the device
///   reset.
///
/// The clip is released elsewhere: when the window is deactivated,
/// `WndProc` handles `WM_ACTIVATE` by calling `ClipCursor` with no
/// rectangle.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Gets the client rectangle of the main window (`ApplicationHWnd`), which
/// Windows reports with its top-left corner at 0,0, converts that corner to
/// screen coordinates with `ClientToScreen`, rebuilds the rectangle at the
/// same width and height from there, and passes it to `ClipCursor`. Return
/// values are not checked. The rectangle is fixed when the call is made; if
/// the window later moves or changes size, the clip keeps the old area
/// until it is changed again, by this function or by `WndProc`'s release
/// on deactivation. On activation `WndProc` also calls the mouse's
/// `setMouseLimits`. BFME 2's base Mouse::setMouseLimits only updates the
/// mouse's own movement limits from the display size and does not touch
/// the Windows clip; whether BFME 2's mouse class overrides it is not
/// confirmed. (In Zero Hour only `DirectInputMouse`, which its game client
/// does not create, overrides it to clip in windowed mode.)
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Both callers clip only in full-screen mode; whether anything confines
/// the cursor in windowed mode is not confirmed. A port that
/// offers borderless or resizable windowed modes has to decide when to
/// clip, must clip again after the window moves or is resized, and should
/// release the clip whenever the game loses focus, as `WndProc` does.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The C++ source is the same as BFME 1's (it was ported from
/// Open-BFME-1), but the compiled code is not byte-identical: BFME 2's
/// retail build compiled it with a different stack frame and saved
/// registers, so the BFME 2 unit uses a different frame setting. BFME 1 has
/// the same two callers. The name comes from Open-BFME-1. Zero Hour's
/// source has no such function and the WorldBuilder twin's copy is unnamed,
/// so the name is not taken from either; the identification rests on the
/// three Windows calls. Zero Hour's closest code is
/// `DirectInputMouse::setMouseLimits`, which clips to the whole window
/// rectangle and only in windowed mode, in a mouse class that Zero Hour's
/// game client leaves unused.
/// \see W3DDisplay::init, Reset_D3D_Device
